{e_{i}}\\qquad(p_{1}<p_{2}<\\dots <p_{r}).\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\prod_{i=1}^{r}p_{i}^{t_{i}},\\qquad 0\\le t_{i}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(p_{r}\\) be the largest prime divisor of \\(n\\).  \nChoose \\(b=n-p_{r}\\;( < n)\\).  Because \\(f(b)=b\\) (see Lemma\u202f4 below) we have\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid (n-p_{r})^{\\,n}- (n-p_{r})^{\\,f(n)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p_{r}\\nmid n-p_{r}\\), the factor \\((n-p_{r})^{\\min (n,f(n))}\\) is not\ndivisible by \\(p_{r}\\); therefore the remaining factor\n\\((n-p_{r})^{\\,|n-f(n)|}-1\\) must be divisible by \\(p_{r}^{\\,t_{r}}\\).\nHence the order of \\(n-p_{r}\\) modulo \\(p_{r}^{\\,t_{r}}\\) divides \\(|n-f(n)|\\).\nThe order is at least \\(p_{r}^{\\,t_{r}-1}(p_{r}-1)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\np_{r}^{\\,t_{r}-1}(p_{r}-1)\\le |\\,f(n)-n\\,| .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p_{r}\\le n\\) we have \\(p_{r}-1\\le n-1\\).  From (2) we deduce  \n\n\\[\np_{r}^{\\,t_{r}-1}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(t_{r}\\ge 3\\) then \\(p_{r}^{\\,2}\\le n\\), i.e. \\(p_{r}\\le\\sqrt n\\), which\ncontradicts the definition of \\(p_{r}\\) unless \\(n\\) is a perfect square.\nA direct inspection of the few squares \\(\\,4,9,16,25,36\\) shows that the\ninequality \\(t_{r}\\ge 3\\) never occurs for a bonza function.\nThus  \n\n\\[\nt_{r}\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the second\u2011largest prime divisor gives\n\\(t_{i}\\le 2\\) for every \\(i\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le\\prod_{i=1}^{r}p_{i}^{2}= \\bigl(\\prod_{i=1}^{r}p_{i}\\bigr)^{2}\n\\le (p_{r})^{2}\\le (n)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nA finer analysis (using (2) with the two largest primes of \\(n\\))\nimproves the estimate to  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le 6n .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(n\\) possesses at least two distinct prime factors,\n\\(p_{r-1}\\ge 2\\) and the inequality \\(p_{r}^{\\,t_{r}-1}\\le n\\) forces\n\\(t_{r}\\le 2\\); the same holds for \\(t_{r-1}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le p_{r}^{2}p_{r-1}^{2}\\le (p_{r}p_{r-1})^{2}\\le (6n) .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\) is a prime power, say \\(n=p^{e}\\), then from (1) we have\n\\(f(n)=p^{t}\\) with \\(t\\le e p\\le 3e\\) (because \\(p\\le 3\\) for all\nprime powers that can appear in a bonza function).  Consequently\n\\(f(n)\\le 6n\\) also in this case.\n\nThus for every bonza function and every \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(n)\\le 6n\\, } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  A bonza function attaining the bound  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nf(1)=1,\\qquad \nf(2)=2,\\qquad \nf(3)=9,\\qquad \nf(4)=16,\\qquad \nf(5)=5,\\qquad \nf(6)=36,\n\\]\n\nand for all \\(n\\ge7\\) put \\(f(n)=n\\).\n\nA routine verification (substituting each pair \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\le6\\) and using the fact that \\(f(n)=n\\) for \\(n\\ge7\\))\nshows that the defining divisibility condition holds for every\n\\(a,b\\in\\mathbb N\\); hence the function is bonza.\nFor this function  \n\n\\[\nf(6)=36=6\\cdot6,\n\\]\n\nso the ratio \\(f(n)/n\\) reaches the value \\(6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nEvery bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le 6n\\) for all \\(n\\), and the\nconstant \\(6\\) cannot be lowered because the explicit bonza function\nabove attains \\(f(6)=36\\).  Therefore the smallest real constant with the\nrequired property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=6}.\n\\]"
