\n2^{e}\\mid b^{\\,n}-f(b)^{\\,2^{e}} .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(b\\) and \\(f(b)\\) are odd, so we may use the **Lifting\u2011the\u2011Exponent Lemma (LTE)**:\n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(b^{\\,n}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+k-1\\ge k+2,\n\\]\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(f(b)^{\\,2^{e}}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(f(b)-1)+v_{2}(f(b)+1)+e-1\\ge e+2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(b^{\\,n}-f(b)^{\\,2^{e}}\\bigr)\n   =\\min\\!\\bigl\\{\\,v_{2}(b^{\\,n}-1),\\,v_{2}(f(b)^{\\,2^{e}}-1)\\bigr\\}\n   \\ge \\min\\{k+2,\\ e+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor (10) to hold we need \\(e\\le k+2\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{e\\le k+2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(n=2^{k}m\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{e}\\le2^{k+2}=4\\cdot2^{k}=4\\frac{n}{m}\\le4n .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (12) occurs exactly when \\(m=1\\) (i.e. \\(n\\) is a power of \\(2\\)) and \\(e=k+2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Extremal bonza function  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nf(1)=1,\\qquad \nf(2)=4,\\qquad \nf(n)=\\begin{cases}\n2^{\\,v_{2}(n)+2}&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\n1&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*If \\(a\\) is odd*, then \\(f(a)=1\\) and (1) is trivial.  \n\n*If \\(a\\) is even*, write \\(a=2^{k}m\\) (\\(m\\) odd) and \\(f(a)=2^{k+2}\\) (or \\(4\\) when \\(a=2\\)).  \nFor any odd \\(b\\) we have \\(f(b)=1\\); by LTE,  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(b^{\\,a}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+k-1\\ge k+2,\n\\]\n\nso \\(2^{k+2}\\mid b^{\\,a}-1\\). Since \\(f(b)^{\\,f(a)}=1\\), the divisibility required in (1) holds.  \n\nFor even \\(b\\) we have \\(f(b)\\) even, hence \\(f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\) is a multiple of \\(2^{f(a)}\\); also \\(b^{\\,a}\\) is a multiple of \\(2^{a}\\ge2^{f(a)}\\). Consequently the difference is divisible by \\(2^{f(a)}\\).  \n\nThus (13) satisfies (1) for all \\(a,b\\); it is a bonza function.  \nFor a power of two, say \\(n=2^{k}\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{k+2}=4\\cdot2^{k}=4n,\n\\]\n\nshowing that the bound \\(f(n)\\le4n\\) is attained.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every bonza function \\(f\\) and every positive integer \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le4n,\n\\]\n\nand the constant \\(4\\) cannot be lowered because the function defined in (13) satisfies \\(f(2^{k})=4\\cdot2^{k}\\). Hence the smallest real constant with the required property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]"
