d\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N .\n   \\]\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The identity function \\(f(n)=n\\) is bonza, attaining the bound \\(f(n)=n\\).  \n   Hence the smallest constant \\(c\\) satisfying \\(f(n)\\le cn\\) for every bonza function is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{c=1}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be a bonza function, i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\text{for all }a,b\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value at \\(1\\)\n\nPut \\(a=1\\) in (★).  Denote \\(k:=f(1)\\).  Then\n\n\\[\nk\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,k}\\qquad\\forall b\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k>1\\) choose a number \\(b\\) with \\(k\\mid f(b)\\) (such a \\(b\\) exists because (1) shows the map \\(b\\mapsto f(b)^{k}\\) is a permutation modulo \\(k\\)).  \nFor this \\(b\\) we have \\(f(b)^{k}\\equiv0\\pmod k\\), hence (1) gives \\(k\\mid b\\).  \nNow apply (★) with \\(a=b\\) and \\(b=1\\):\n\n\\[\nf(b)\\mid 1^{\\,b}-f(1)^{\\,f(b)}=1-k^{\\,f(b)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\mid f(b)\\), the right‑hand side is congruent to \\(1\\pmod k\\), which is impossible because the left‑hand side is a multiple of \\(k\\).  \nThus \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(1\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(1)=1}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A first divisibility\n\nTake \\(b=a\\) in (★).  Because \\(f(a)\\mid a^{a}-f(a)^{\\,f(a)}\\) and the second term is a multiple of \\(f(a)\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a)\\mid a^{a}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence every prime divisor of \\(f(a)\\) also divides \\(a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimal counter‑example\n\nAssume that the set  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{n\\in\\mathbb N\\mid f(n)>n\\}\n\\]\n\nis non‑empty and let  \n\n\\[\nn_{0}:=\\min S .\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(m<n_{0}\\) we have \\(f(m)\\le m\\).  \nFrom (2) we know that each prime factor of \\(f(n_{0})\\) is \\(\\le n_{0}\\).  \nBecause \\(f(n_{0})>n_{0}\\), some prime \\(p\\le n_{0}\\) occurs in the factorisation of \\(f(n_{0})\\) with exponent at least \\(2\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\np^{2}\\mid f(n_{0}). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Determining \\(f(p)\\)\n\nApply (★) with \\(a=n_{0}\\) and \\(b=p\\):\n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})\\mid p^{\\,n_{0}}-f(p)^{\\,f(n_{0})}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nReducing (4) modulo \\(p^{2}\\) and using (3) yields  \n\n\\[\np^{2}\\mid f(p)^{\\,f(n_{0})}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(p\\mid f(p)\\).  Since \\(p<n_{0}\\), the minimality of \\(n_{0}\\) gives \\(f(p)\\le p\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(p)=p}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A congruence for \\(f(n_{0})\\)\n\nNow use (★) with \\(a=p\\) and \\(b=n_{0}\\).  By (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\np\\mid n_{0}^{\\,p}-f(n_{0})^{\\,p}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nReducing (6) modulo \\(p\\) and invoking Fermat’s theorem (\\(x^{p}\\equiv x\\pmod p\\) for any integer \\(x\\)) gives\n\n\\[\np\\mid n_{0}-f(n_{0}),\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad f(n_{0})\\equiv n_{0}\\pmod p . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Consequences of (3) and (7)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})=p^{2}t,\\qquad n_{0}=p\\,s\\qquad(t,s\\in\\mathbb N).\n\\]\n\nEquation (7) becomes \\(p^{2}t\\equiv p s\\pmod p\\), which is automatically true; however (7) together with \\(p^{2}\\mid f(n_{0})\\) forces \\(p\\mid n_{0}\\), i.e. \\(s\\) is an integer.  \nBecause \\(f(n_{0})>n_{0}\\), we have \\(p^{2}t>p s\\), whence \\(t\\ge 1\\) and \\(s\\le p t\\).  \nIn particular \\(n_{0}=p s\\ge p^{2}\\).  \n\nNow (2) tells us that \\(f(n_{0})\\mid n_{0}^{\\,n_{0}}\\).  \nThe smallest multiple of \\(p^{2}\\) exceeding \\(n_{0}\\) is \\(p^{2}\\) itself; therefore the only way for a multiple of \\(p^{2}\\) to be \\(\\le n_{0}^{\\,n_{0}}\\) and larger than \\(n_{0}\\) is that\n\n\\[\nf(n_{0})=p^{2}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad n_{0}=p^{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final contradiction\n\nTake \\(a=n_{0}=p^{2}\\) and \\(b=p^{2}+1\\) in (★).  \nUsing (2) we have \\(p^{2}\\mid (p^{2}+1)^{p^{2}}-f(p^{2}+1)^{\\,p^{2}}\\).  \nModulo \\(p^{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n(p^{2}+1)^{p^{2}}\\equiv 1^{p^{2}}=1,\\qquad\nf(p^{2}+1)^{\\,p^{2}}\\equiv f(p^{2}+1)^{\\,p^{2}}\\pmod{p^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(p^{2}\\mid 1-f(p^{2}+1)^{\\,p^{2}}\\).  \nIf \\(p\\mid f(p^{2}+1)\\) then \\(p^{2}\\mid f(p^{2}+1)^{\\,p^{2}}\\) and the congruence would give \\(p^{2}\\mid1\\), impossible.  \nThus \\(p\\nmid f(p^{2}+1)\\).  But by (2) every prime divisor of \\(f(p^{2}+1)\\) must divide \\(p^{2}+1\\), which is coprime to \\(p\\).  Consequently \\(f(p^{2}+1)\\) is coprime to \\(p\\) and also to \\(p^{2}\\).  \nTherefore \\(f(p^{2}+1)^{\\,p^{2}}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{2}}\\), and the required divisibility again forces \\(p^{2}\\mid0\\), a contradiction.\n\nHence our assumption that \\(S\\neq\\varnothing\\) is false.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Upper bound for all bonza functions\n\nWe have proved that no integer \\(n\\) can satisfy \\(f(n)>n\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\le n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N } .\n\\]\n\nThe identity function \\(f(n)=n\\) satisfies the defining condition (★) because the right‑hand side is identically zero; thus the bound is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Determination of the constant \\(c\\)\n\nSince every bonza function obeys \\(f(n)\\le n\\) and the function \\(f(n)=n\\) attains equality, the smallest real constant \\(c\\) with the property “\\(f(n)\\le cn\\) for all bonza functions and all \\(n\\)” is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
