                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem. The smallest real constant \\(c\\) with the property  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le cn\\qquad\\text{for every bonza function }f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\text{ and every }n\\in\\mathbb N\n\\]\n\nis  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=2}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Basic divisibility.**  \n   From the defining condition with \\(a=b\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   f(a)\\mid a^{a}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every prime divisor of \\(f(a)\\) also divides \\(a\\).\n\n2. **Odd arguments.**  \n   Using \\(a=p\\) (odd prime) and \\(b=2\\) together with the fact that \\(f(2)\\mid2^{2}=4\\) we prove  \n\n   \\[\n   f(p)=1\\qquad\\forall\\text{ odd prime }p.\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently, for any odd integer \\(n\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=1.\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Even arguments are powers of 2.**  \n   Let \\(n=2^{\\alpha}m\\) with \\(m\\) odd (\\(\\alpha\\ge1\\)).  \n   By (1) all prime divisors of \\(f(n)\\) divide \\(n\\); (2)–(3) exclude odd primes, so  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=2^{\\beta}\\qquad(\\beta\\ge0).\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Upper bound for the exponent.**  \n   Choose any odd integer \\(b\\).  From the defining condition with this \\(b\\) and the fact that \\(f(b)=1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   2^{\\beta}\\mid b^{\\,n}-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Applying the Lifting‑the‑Exponent Lemma (LTE) for odd \\(b\\) and even \\(n\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   v_{2}\\!\\bigl(b^{\\,n}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+\\alpha-1\\ge\\alpha+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\beta\\le\\alpha+1\\) and therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=2^{\\beta}\\le2^{\\alpha+1}=2\\cdot2^{\\alpha}\\le2n.\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sharpness.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   1, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   2^{\\nu_{2}(n)+1}, & n\\text{ even},\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\qquad\\bigl(\\nu_{2}(n)=\\text{exponent of }2\\text{ in }n\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   A direct verification (splitting according to the parity of \\(a\\)) shows that this function satisfies the bonza condition for all \\(a,b\\).  For a power of two, say \\(n=2^{k}\\), we have \\(f(n)=2^{k+1}=2n\\); thus the ratio \\(f(n)/n\\) can be arbitrarily close to \\(2\\).  Consequently the constant \\(2\\) cannot be improved.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le2n\\) for all \\(n\\), and the example above shows that the bound \\(2\\) is attained.  Hence the smallest admissible constant is \\(c=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf:\\mathbb N\\longrightarrow\\mathbb N\n\\]\n\nbe a bonza function, i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\n\n*Setting \\(b=a\\) in (★) gives*\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid a^{a}-f(a)^{\\,f(a)} .\n\\]\n\nSince the second term on the right is a multiple of \\(f(a)\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a)\\mid a^{a}}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every prime divisor of \\(f(a)\\) divides \\(a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Values at odd arguments\n\nFirst we determine \\(f(2)\\).  From (1) we have \\(f(2)\\mid2^{2}=4\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nf(2)\\in\\{1,2,4\\}.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(p\\) be an odd prime.  Apply (★) with \\(a=p\\) and \\(b=2\\).  \nUsing (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nf(p)\\mid2^{p}-f(2)^{\\,f(p)} .\n\\]\n\nWe treat the three possibilities for \\(f(2)\\).\n\n*If \\(f(2)=1\\):* the right‑hand side equals \\(2^{p}-1\\).  \nModulo \\(p\\) we have \\(2^{p}\\equiv2\\) (Fermat), so \\(2^{p}-1\\equiv1\\pmod p\\); thus \\(p\\nmid2^{p}-1\\).  \nBecause any prime divisor of \\(f(p)\\) must be \\(p\\) (by (1)), we conclude \\(f(p)=1\\).\n\n*If \\(f(2)=2\\):* the right‑hand side is \\(2^{p}-2^{\\,f(p)}=2^{\\,f(p)}\\!\\bigl(2^{p-f(p)}-1\\bigr)\\).  \nThe factor in parentheses is odd, so the whole expression is a power of \\(2\\).  \nAgain any odd prime divisor of \\(f(p)\\) would have to be \\(p\\), which cannot divide a power of \\(2\\); hence \\(f(p)=1\\).\n\n*If \\(f(2)=4\\):* the right‑hand side is \\(2^{p}-4^{\\,f(p)}=2^{\\,f(p)}\\!\\bigl(2^{p-f(p)}-2^{\\,f(p)}\\bigr)\\).  \nThe bracket is even, but its odd part is \\(1\\); thus the whole number is a power of \\(2\\).  \nThe same argument as above forces \\(f(p)=1\\).\n\nTherefore for every odd prime \\(p\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(p)=1}.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(n\\) be an arbitrary odd integer and let \\(p\\) be any odd prime divisor of \\(n\\).  \nApplying (★) with \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=p\\) and using (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid p^{\\,n}-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\mid n\\), we have \\(p^{\\,n}\\equiv0\\pmod p\\), whence \\(p\\nmid p^{\\,n}-1\\).  \nAll prime divisors of \\(f(n)\\) divide \\(n\\) (by (1)), thus they are among the primes dividing \\(p\\); but none of them divides the right‑hand side.  Consequently \\(f(n)\\) has no prime divisor and must be \\(1\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=1\\qquad\\forall\\;n\\text{ odd}}.\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Even arguments are powers of 2\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even and write  \n\n\\[\nn=2^{\\alpha}m,\\qquad \\alpha\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd}.\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) every prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) divides \\(n\\).  \nIf an odd prime \\(q\\) divided \\(f(n)\\), then \\(q\\mid m\\).  \nApplying (★) with \\(a=m\\) (odd) and \\(b=n\\) and using (4) we would have  \n\n\\[\n1=f(m)\\mid n^{\\,m}-f(n)^{\\,1}=n^{\\,m}-f(n),\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because the left‑hand side is \\(1\\) while the right‑hand side is even.  \nThus no odd prime divides \\(f(n)\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=2^{\\beta}\\quad\\text{for some }\\beta\\ge0.}\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Bounding the exponent \\(\\beta\\)\n\nChoose any odd integer \\(b\\) (for instance \\(b=3\\)).  \nSince \\(b\\) is odd, (4) gives \\(f(b)=1\\).  Applying (★) with this \\(b\\) yields\n\n\\[\n2^{\\beta}=f(n)\\mid b^{\\,n}-1 .\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(n=2^{\\alpha}m\\) as above.  Because \\(b\\) is odd, the Lifting‑the‑Exponent Lemma (LTE) for the prime \\(2\\) gives\n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\!\\bigl(b^{\\,n}-1\\bigr)\n   =v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+v_{2}(n)-1\n   =v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+\\alpha-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any odd \\(b\\) the two consecutive even numbers \\(b-1\\) and \\(b+1\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)\\ge2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\!\\bigl(b^{\\,n}-1\\bigr)\\ge\\alpha+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2^{\\beta}\\mid b^{\\,n}-1\\), we must have \\(\\beta\\le v_{2}(b^{\\,n}-1)\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\beta\\le\\alpha+1}.\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(n=2^{\\alpha}m\\ge2^{\\alpha}\\) we obtain from (5)–(6)\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{\\beta}\\le2^{\\alpha+1}=2\\cdot2^{\\alpha}\\le2n .\n\\]\n\nThus **every bonza function satisfies**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\le2n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N}.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Sharpness of the bound\n\nDefine a function \\(g:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) by  \n\n\\[\ng(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n2^{\\nu_{2}(n)+1}, & n\\text{ even},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\\bigl(\\nu_{2}(n)=\\text{exponent of }2\\text{ in }n\\bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(g\\) is bonza.\n\n*Case \\(a\\) odd.*  Then \\(g(a)=1\\) and (★) is trivially satisfied.\n\n*Case \\(a\\) even.*  Write \\(a=2^{\\alpha}u\\) with \\(u\\) odd (\\(\\alpha\\ge1\\)); then \\(g(a)=2^{\\alpha+1}\\).  \nLet \\(b=2^{\\beta}v\\) with \\(v\\) odd (\\(\\beta\\ge0\\)).  \n\n- If \\(\\beta=0\\) (i.e. \\(b\\) odd), then \\(g(b)=1\\) and we must show  \n\n  \\[\n  2^{\\alpha+1}\\mid v^{\\,2^{\\alpha}u}-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  By LTE (as in Section 4)  \n\n  \\[\n  v_{2}\\!\\bigl(v^{\\,2^{\\alpha}u}-1\\bigr)\n   =v_{2}(v-1)+v_{2}(v+1)+\\alpha-1\\ge\\alph