{k+2}}\\) are multiples of \\(2^{k+2}\\) because \\(ta\\ge 2^{k}\\ge k+2\\) for \\(k\\ge2\\).  \n     Hence their difference is also a multiple of \\(2^{k+2}=f(a)\\).\n\n   Thus (5) satisfies the defining condition, and for every power of two \\(n=2^{k}\\;(k\\ge2)\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   f(n)=2^{k+2}=4n,\n   \\]\n   showing that the constant \\(4\\) cannot be lowered.\n\nSince every bonza function obeys \\(f(n)\\le 4n\\) and the function defined in (5) attains the ratio \\(f(n)/n=4\\) for infinitely many \\(n\\), the smallest possible constant is \\(c=4\\).\n\n--- \n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be bonza; i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary consequences\n\n*Putting \\(b=a\\) in (1) gives*  \n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid a^{a}-f(a)^{\\,f(a)}\\equiv a^{a}\\pmod{f(a)},\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a)\\mid a^{a}}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Putting \\(a=1\\) in (1) yields*  \n\n\\[\nf(1)\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\qquad\\forall b .\n\\]\n\nTaking \\(b=1\\) we obtain \\(f(1)\\mid 1-f(1)^{\\,f(1)}\\).  Since the right‑hand side is\n\\(1\\) modulo \\(f(1)\\) unless \\(f(1)=1\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(1)=1}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Behaviour on odd arguments\n\nLet \\(b\\) be odd.  Because any divisor of an odd integer is odd, (2) implies that\n\\(f(b)\\) is odd.  Consequently \\(f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\equiv 1\\pmod{f(a)}\\), and (1) reduces to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(a)\\mid b^{a}-1}\\qquad\\forall a\\in\\mathbb N,\\;\\forall\\;b\\text{ odd}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(b\\) the Lifting‑the‑Exponent Lemma gives  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(b^{a}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+v_{2}(a)-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince one of \\(b-1,b+1\\) is divisible by \\(4\\) and the other by \\(2\\), we have\n\\(v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)\\ge 3\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(b^{a}-1\\bigr)\\ge v_{2}(a)+2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid 2^{\\,v_{2}(a)+2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(a)\\le 2^{\\,v_{2}(a)+2}=4\\cdot2^{\\,v_{2}(a)} .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a=2^{k}m\\) with \\(m\\) odd (\\(k=v_{2}(a)\\)).  Then (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(a)\\le 4\\cdot2^{k}\\le 4a . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus **every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le 4n\\) for all \\(n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Sharpness of the bound – a construction\n\nDefine \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) by  \n\n\\[\nf(1)=1,\\qquad \nf(2)=4,\\qquad \nf(n)=\\begin{cases}\n1, & n\\text{ odd }(n>1),\\\\[2mm]\n2^{\\,v_{2}(n)+2}, & n\\text{ even }(n\\ge 4).\n\\end{cases} \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*Verification of (1).*\n\n*Case 1 – \\(a\\) odd.*  \nThen \\(f(a)=1\\) and (1) holds trivially.\n\n*Case 2 – \\(a\\) even.*  \nWrite \\(a=2^{k}m\\) with \\(k\\ge2\\) (the case \\(a=2\\) is covered by the explicit value\n\\(f(2)=4\\)).  Hence \\(f(a)=2^{k+2}\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.1 – \\(b\\) odd.*  \nBecause \\(f(b)=1\\), the right‑hand side of (1) is \\(b^{a}-1\\).  \nBy (6) we have \\(v_{2}(b^{a}-1)\\ge k+2\\); therefore \\(2^{k+2}=f(a)\\) divides \\(b^{a}-1\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.2 – \\(b\\) even.*  \nWrite \\(b=2^{t}n\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\) odd.  Then \\(f(b)=2^{t+2}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nb^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\n=2^{ta}n^{a}-2^{(t+2)2^{k+2}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth summands are multiples of \\(2^{k+2}\\) because \\(ta\\ge 2^{k}\\ge k+2\\) for\n\\(k\\ge2\\).  Consequently their difference is also a multiple of \\(2^{k+2}=f(a)\\).\n\nThus (1) holds for all \\(a,b\\); the function (9) is bonza.\n\nFor every power of two \\(n=2^{k}\\;(k\\ge2)\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{k+2}=4n .\n\\]\n\nHence the ratio \\(f(n)/n\\) attains the value \\(4\\) infinitely often, showing that\nthe constant \\(4\\) cannot be reduced.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nEvery bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le 4n\\) for all positive integers \\(n\\),\nand there exists a bonza function for which \\(f(n)=4n\\) for infinitely many\n\\(n\\).  Therefore the smallest real constant with the required property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]"
