a power of \\(p\\); consequently the exponent \\(v\\) cannot exceed the exponent of \\(p\\) in \\(n\\), i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   v\\le t=v_{p}(n).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion of the bound**  \n   Inequality (2) holds for every prime \\(p\\) dividing \\(f(n)\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(f(n))}\\;\\le\\;\n   \\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(n)}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le n\\) for all \\(n\\in\\mathbb N\\).\n\n5. **Sharpness**  \n   The function \\(f(n)=n\\) clearly satisfies the defining condition because  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\mid b^{\\,n}-f(b)^{\\,n}=b^{\\,n}-b^{\\,n}=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the bound \\(f(n)\\le n\\) is best possible, and the smallest constant \\(c\\) with the required property is \\(c=1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be bonza; i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\text{for all }a,b\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary facts\n\n*Put \\(a=1\\) in (\u2605).*\n\n\\[\nf(1)\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\qquad(\\forall b).\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(b=1\\) gives \\(f(1)\\mid 1-1^{\\,f(1)}=0\\); hence \\(f(1)=1\\).\n\n*Put \\(a=n,\\;b=n\\) in (\u2605).*\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}-f(n)^{\\,f(n)} .\n\\]\n\nSince the second term is a multiple of \\(f(n)\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}\\qquad(\\forall n). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) also divides \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A key lemma\n\nLet \\(p\\) be a prime and let  \n\n\\[\nv=v_{p}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\;(v\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nb=1+p^{\\,v+1}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\gcd(b,n)=1\\) (because \\(p\\mid f(n)\\) implies \\(p\\mid n\\) by (1), but \\(b\\equiv1\\pmod p\\)).  \nBy the binomial theorem,\n\n\\[\nb^{\\,n}= (1+p^{\\,v+1})^{n}\\equiv 1 \\pmod{p^{\\,v+1}} . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApplying (\u2605) with this \\(a=n\\) and the chosen \\(b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid b^{\\,n}-f(b)^{\\,f(n)} .\n\\]\n\nReducing modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\) and using (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(b)^{\\,f(n)}\\equiv 1 \\pmod{p^{\\,v+1}} . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), the prime \\(p\\) does **not** divide \\(b\\); hence \\(p\\nmid f(b)\\) (otherwise \\(p\\mid b\\) by (\u2605) with \\(a=1\\), contradicting \\(f(1)=1\\)).  \nThus \\(f(b)\\) is a unit modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\).\n\nLet \\(\\operatorname{ord}_{p^{\\,v+1}}(f(b))\\) denote the multiplicative order of \\(f(b)\\) modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\).  \nEquation (3) tells us that this order divides \\(f(n)\\).  \nOn the other hand, for any unit modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\) the order also divides Euler\u2019s totient  \n\n\\[\n\\varphi(p^{\\,v+1})=p^{\\,v}(p-1).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid p^{\\,v}(p-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Bounding the exponent of a prime in \\(f(n)\\)\n\nWrite the prime\u2011power factorisation of \\(n\\) as  \n\n\\[\nn=p^{\\,t}m,\\qquad p\\nmid m .\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we already know \\(v\\le nt\\); we now improve this bound.\n\nBecause \\(p\\mid f(n)\\) we have \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nTaking \\(p\\)-adic valuations in (4) gives  \n\n\\[\nv_{p}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)=v\\le v_{p}\\bigl(p^{\\,v}(p-1)\\bigr)=v .\n\\]\n\nThus (4) forces the factor \\(p-1\\) to contribute **no** extra power of \\(p\\); consequently the only way (4) can be satisfied is when  \n\n\\[\nv\\le t=v_{p}(n). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInequality (5) holds for **every** prime \\(p\\) dividing \\(f(n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The global bound \\(f(n)\\le n\\)\n\nUsing (5) for all primes we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(f(n))}\n      \\le\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(n)}=n .\n\\]\n\nHence every bonza function satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(n)\\le n\\quad\\text{for all }n\\in\\mathbb N\\,}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Sharpness of the bound\n\nThe function  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=n\\qquad(\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N)\n\\]\n\nclearly fulfills the defining condition: for any \\(a,b\\),\n\n\\[\nf(a)=a\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,a}=b^{a}-b^{a}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus (6) is best possible.  \n\nConsequently the smallest real constant \\(c\\) such that \\(f(n)\\le cn\\) holds for **all** bonza functions is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
