"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that every bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le n\\) for all positive integers \\(n\\).  \nSince the identity function \\(f(n)=n\\) is bonza, the smallest real constant \\(c\\) for which  \n\\(f(n)\\le cn\\) holds for every bonza function is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Immediate consequences of the definition**  \n   * From the case \\(a=1\\) we obtain \\(f(1)=1\\).  \n   * From the case \\(a=n,\\;b=n\\) we obtain \\(f(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}\\); consequently every prime divisor of \\(f(n)\\) also divides \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Key Lemma (prime\u2011power part)**  \n   Let \\(p\\) be a prime and let \\(v=v_{p}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\).  \n   Choose \\(b=1+p^{\\,v+1}\\). Then \\(p\\nmid b\\) and, because any prime dividing \\(f(b)\\) must also divide \\(b\\), we have \\(p\\nmid f(b)\\).  \n   Using the bonza condition with this \\(b\\) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid b^{\\,n}-f(b)^{\\,f(n)} .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(b^{\\,n}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{\\,v+1}}\\), reduction modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   f(b)^{\\,f(n)}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{\\,v+1}} .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(p\\nmid f(b)\\), the multiplicative order of \\(f(b)\\) modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\) divides both \\(f(n)\\) and \\(\\varphi(p^{\\,v+1})=p^{\\,v}(p-1)\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\mid p^{\\,v}(p-1). \\tag{L}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bounding the exponent of a prime in \\(f(n)\\)**  \n   Write \\(n=p^{\\,t}m\\) with \\(p\\nmid m\\).  \n   From step\u202f1 we already have \\(f(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}=p^{\\,tn}m^{\\,n}\\); therefore the \\(p\\)\u2011adic valuation of \\(f(n)\\) is at most \\(tn\\).  \n   Combining this with (L) we compare the \\(p\\)\u2011adic valuations of the two divisibilities: the right\u2011hand side of (L) contains exactly \\(p^{\\,v}\\) as its \\(p\\)\u2011part (the factor \\(p-1\\) is coprime to \\(p\\)). Hence the \\(p\\)\u2011part of \\(f(n)\\) is precisely \\(p^{\\,v}\\).  \n   Since the same \\(p\\)\u2011part must also divide \\(p^{\\,tn}\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\le t=v_{p}(n). \\tag{*}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Global bound**  \n   Inequality \\((*)\\) holds for every prime \\(p\\) dividing \\(f(n)\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(f(n))}\n        \\le\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(n)}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus every bonza function satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   f(n)\\le n\\qquad(\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N). \\tag{**}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sharpness**  \n   The function \\(f(n)=n\\) fulfills the defining condition because for any \\(a,b\\),\n\n   \\[\n   f(a)=a\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,a}=b^{a}-b^{a}=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the bound \\((**)\\) cannot be improved, and the smallest constant \\(c\\) with the required property is \\(c=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(f:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\) be a *bonza* function, i.e.\n\n\\[\nf(a)\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\qquad\\text{for all }a,b\\in\\mathbb N .\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary facts\n\n*Put \\(a=1\\) in (\u2605).*  \nThen \\(f(1)\\mid b-f(b)^{\\,f(1)}\\) for every \\(b\\).  \nChoosing \\(b=1\\) gives \\(f(1)\\mid 1-f(1)^{\\,f(1)}\\).  \nSince \\(f(1)\\mid f(1)^{\\,f(1)}\\), the divisibility forces \\(f(1)=1\\).\n\n*Put \\(a=n,\\;b=n\\) in (\u2605).*  \nWe obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}-f(n)^{\\,f(n)} .\n\\]\n\nThe second term on the right is a multiple of \\(f(n)\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}\\qquad(\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, if a prime \\(p\\) divides \\(f(n)\\) then \\(p\\mid n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A key lemma\n\nFix a prime \\(p\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nv=v_{p}\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)\\;(v\\ge0).\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nb=1+p^{\\,v+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\equiv1\\pmod p\\), we have \\(p\\nmid b\\).  \nIf \\(p\\mid f(b)\\) then, by (1), \\(p\\mid b\\), a contradiction; hence  \n\n\\[\np\\nmid f(b). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApply (\u2605) with this \\(b\\) and \\(a=n\\):\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid b^{\\,n}-f(b)^{\\,f(n)} .\n\\]\n\nThe binomial theorem gives  \n\n\\[\nb^{\\,n}=(1+p^{\\,v+1})^{n}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{\\,v+1}} . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nReducing the previous divisibility modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\) and using (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(b)^{\\,f(n)}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{\\,v+1}} . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\nmid f(b)\\) by (2), the multiplicative order of \\(f(b)\\) modulo \\(p^{\\,v+1}\\) divides both \\(f(n)\\) and Euler\u2019s totient  \n\n\\[\n\\varphi(p^{\\,v+1})=p^{\\,v}(p-1).\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(n)\\mid p^{\\,v}(p-1)\\,}. \\tag{L}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Bounding the exponent of \\(p\\) in \\(f(n)\\)\n\nWrite the factorisation of \\(n\\) as  \n\n\\[\nn=p^{\\,t}m,\\qquad p\\nmid m .\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(f(n)\\mid n^{\\,n}=p^{\\,tn}m^{\\,n}\\); hence the \\(p\\)\u2011adic valuation of \\(f(n)\\) does not exceed \\(tn\\).  \nRelation (L) tells us that the \\(p\\)\u2011part of \\(f(n)\\) is exactly \\(p^{\\,v}\\) (the factor \\(p-1\\) is coprime to \\(p\\)).  \nThus the same \\(p^{\\,v}\\) must also divide the \\(p\\)-part of \\(n^{\\,n}\\), namely \\(p^{\\,tn}\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nv\\le tn .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge p^{\\,t}\\), the inequality \\(v\\le tn\\) forces  \n\n\\[\nv\\le t=v_{p}(n). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(v>t\\) then \\(p^{\\,v}\\) would exceed the highest power of \\(p\\) that can appear in any divisor of \\(p^{\\,tn}\\) having the form \\(p^{\\,v}(p-1)\\), contradicting (L). Hence (5) holds for every prime \\(p\\) dividing \\(f(n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The global bound\n\nUsing (5) for all primes we obtain  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(f(n))}\n      \\le\\prod_{p\\mid n}p^{\\,v_{p}(n)}=n .\n\\]\n\nThus every bonza function satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\le n\\qquad(\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N)}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Sharpness of the bound\n\nConsider the function \\(f(n)=n\\).  \nFor any positive integers \\(a,b\\),\n\n\\[\nf(a)=a\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,a}=b^{a}-b^{a}=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(f\\) is bonza, and equality holds in (6).  \nTherefore the inequality \\(f(n)\\le n\\) cannot be improved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Determination of the constant \\(c\\)\n\nFrom (6) we have \\(f(n)\\le n\\) for every bonza function, i.e. the statement holds with \\(c=1\\).  \nSince the bonza function \\(f(n)=n\\) attains the ratio \\(f(n)/n=1\\), no smaller constant works.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{c=1}.\n\\]"
