n.\n\n- Step 3: Lemma: fix a prime p dividing f(n). Let v = v_p(f(n)). Choose b = 1 + p^{v+1}. Since b ≡ 1 mod p, p does not divide b. If p divides f(b), then by (1) (the earlier result f(b) | b^b), p divides b, contradiction. So p does not divide f(b). Good.\n\nNow apply (★) with a=n, b = 1 + p^{v+1}: f(n) divides b^n - f(b)^{f(n)}. Then b^n = (1 + p^{v+1})^n ≡ 1 (mod p^{v+1}) by binomial theorem because all terms except the first have factor p^{v+1}. So b^n ≡ 1 (mod p^{v+1}). Then from divisibility f(n) | b^n - f(b)^{f(n)}, we have b^n - f(b)^{f(n)} ≡ 0 (mod f(n)). But we want to reduce modulo p^{v+1}. Since f(n) is divisible by p^v but not necessarily p^{v+1}, we need to consider the congruence modulo p^{v+1}. The solution says reducing the divisibility modulo p^{v+1} and using (3) we obtain f(b)^{f(n)} ≡ 1 (mod p^{v+1}). Let's check: Since f(n) divides b^n - f(b)^{f(n)}, there exists integer k such that b^n - f(b)^{f(n)} = k f(n). Taking modulo p^{v+1}, we have b^n - f(b)^{f(n)} ≡ k f(n) (mod p^{v+1}). Since f(n) is divisible by p^v, we have f(n) = p^v * u where p ∤ u. Then k f(n) ≡ k p^v u (mod p^{v+1}). This is a multiple of p^v but not necessarily of p^{v+1}. However, we also have b^n ≡ 1 (mod p^{v+1}). So we get 1 - f(b)^{f(n)} ≡ k p^v u (mod p^{v+1}). This does not directly imply f(b)^{f(n)} ≡ 1 (mod p^{v+1}) unless we know that k p^v u ≡ 0 (mod p^{v+1}), i.e., p^{v+1} divides k p^v u, which would require p divides k u. Since p ∤ u, we need p | k. Is that guaranteed? Not obviously. However, we might argue that since f(n) divides the difference, the difference is a multiple of f(n) = p^v u. So the difference is divisible by p^v, but we need it to be divisible by p^{v+1} to deduce the congruence. The solution claims that because b^n ≡ 1 (mod p^{v+1}) and the difference is divisible by f(n), we get f(b)^{f(n)} ≡ 1 (mod p^{v+1}). This step may be flawed unless we can argue that p^{v+1} divides f(n) (which is false) or that the difference is divisible by p^{v+1}