s both f(n) and φ(p^v). So the order divides gcd(f(n), p^{v-1}(p-1)). This does not directly give f(n) | p^v(p-1). However, we can still bound v: Since p^v divides f(n), we have v ≤ v_p(f(n)). But we need to bound v in terms of v_p(n). The solution eventually uses v ≤ v_p(n). Let's see if we can prove that without the questionable step.\n\nAlternative approach: Since f(n) | n^n, we have v ≤ n * v_p(n). That's a trivial bound. But we need a stronger bound v ≤ v_p(n). The solution uses the lemma to get f(n) | p^v(p-1). Then since p^v(p-1) divides n^n, we get p^v divides n^n, but also p does not divide p-1, so p^v must divide n^n. But n^n has p-adic valuation n * v_p(n). So v ≤ n * v_p(n). That's not enough to get v ≤ v_p(n). The solution then argues that because v ≤ t (where t = v_p(n)), else p^v would exceed the highest power of p that can appear in any divisor of p^{tn} of the form p^v(p-1). This argument is not rigorous.\n\nWe need to examine the lemma more carefully. The lemma states: f(n) | p^v(p-1). Let's verify if that holds. The lemma's derivation is suspect because of the step (4). Let's try to salvage: Perhaps we can prove a stronger statement: For any prime p dividing f(n), we have p-1 divisible by f(n)/p^v. Actually, the lemma says f(n) divides p^v(p-1). This would imply that the part of f(n) coprime to p divides p-1. So the p'-part of f(n) is bounded by p-1. That is plausible: Since p ∤ f(b), the order of f(b) modulo p^{v+1} divides φ(p^{v+1}) = p^v(p-1). If we can show that f(n) divides the order (or a multiple of it), we get the result. But we need to show that f(n) divides the order of f(b) modulo p^{v+1}. Actually, we have f(b)^{f(n)} ≡ b^n (mod f(n)). But we need modulo p^{v+1}. Not straightforward.\n\nThus the lemma may be flawed.\n\nBut perhaps there is an alternative approach to prove f(n) ≤ n without the lemma. Let's see if the solution's conclusion is correct anyway. The claim that f(n) ≤ n for all bonbon functions may be true. Let's try to see 