odd, \\(p\\mid m\\). As this holds for every odd prime divisor of \\(d\\), we conclude \\(d\\mid m\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. 2\u2011adic bound for \\(f(n)\\)\n\n**Lemma 2.**\u2003\\(e\\le k+2\\).\n\n*Proof.* Choose the odd integer \\(b=3\\). By (3) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(3)=3^{v}\\qquad\\text{with }v\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\]\n\nApplying (1) with \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=3\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid3^{\\,n}-\\bigl(3^{v}\\bigr)^{\\,f(n)}=3^{\\,n}-3^{\\,v f(n)}.\n\\]\n\nFactorising the right\u2011hand side gives  \n\n\\[\n3^{\\,\\min(n,vf(n))}\\bigl(3^{\\,|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe first factor is odd, so the 2\u2011adic valuation of the whole expression equals the 2\u2011adic valuation of the second factor.  \nFor an odd base the Lifting\u2011the\u2011Exponent Lemma states\n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\!\\bigl(3^{t}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(3-1)+v_{2}(3+1)+v_{2}(t)-1=2+v_{2}(t) \\qquad(t\\ge1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(3^{\\,|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|}-1\\bigr)=2+v_{2}\\!\\bigl(|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f(n)=2^{e}d\\) divides the expression, its 2\u2011adic valuation cannot exceed the right\u2011hand side of (5); therefore\n\n\\[\ne\\le2+v_{2}\\!\\bigl(|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(vf(n)=v\\cdot2^{e}d\\) is a multiple of \\(2^{e}\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nn-vf(n)\\equiv n\\pmod{2^{e}}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(e>k\\) then \\(v_{2}\\!\\bigl(|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|\\bigr)=v_{2}(n)=k\\); inserting this into (6) gives \\(e\\le2+k\\).  \nIf \\(e\\le k\\) the inequality \\(e\\le k+2\\) is already true.  \nThus in all cases  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{e\\le k+2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Universal linear bound\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(d\\mid m\\), whence \\(d\\le m\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(2^{e}\\le2^{k+2}\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{e}d\\le2^{k+2}m=4\\cdot2^{k}m=4n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N. \\tag