odd, \\(p\\mid m\\). As this holds for every odd prime divisor of \\(d\\), we conclude \\(d\\mid m\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. 2\u2011adic bound for \\(f(n)\\)\n\n**Lemma 2.**\u2003\\(e\\le k+2\\).\n\n*Proof.* Choose the odd integer \\(b=3\\). By (3) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(3)=3^{v}\\qquad\\text{with }v\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\]\n\nApplying (1) with \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=3\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\mid3^{\\,n}-\\bigl(3^{v}\\bigr)^{\\,f(n)}=3^{\\,n}-3^{\\,v f(n)}.\n\\]\n\nFactorising the right\u2011hand side gives  \n\n\\[\n3^{\\,\\min(n,vf(n))}\\bigl(3^{\\,|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe first factor is odd, so the 2\u2011adic valuation of the whole expression equals the 2\u2011adic valuation of the second factor.  \nFor an odd base the Lifting\u2011the\u2011Exponent Lemma states\n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\!\\bigl(3^{t}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(3-1)+v_{2}(3+1)+v_{2}(t)-1=2+v_{2}(t) \\qquad(t\\ge1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(3^{\\,|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|}-1\\bigr)=2+v_{2}\\!\\bigl(|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f(n)=2^{e}d\\) divides the expression, its 2\u2011adic valuation cannot exceed the right\u2011hand side of (5); therefore\n\n\\[\ne\\le2+v_{2}\\!\\bigl(|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(vf(n)=v\\cdot2^{e}d\\) is a multiple of \\(2^{e}\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nn-vf(n)\\equiv n\\pmod{2^{e}}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(e>k\\) then \\(v_{2}\\!\\bigl(|\\,n-vf(n)\\,|\\bigr)=v_{2}(n)=k\\); inserting this into (6) gives \\(e\\le2+k\\).  \nIf \\(e\\le k\\) the inequality \\(e\\le k+2\\) is already true.  \nThus in all cases  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{e\\le k+2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Universal linear bound\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(d\\mid m\\), whence \\(d\\le m\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(2^{e}\\le2^{k+2}\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2^{e}d\\le2^{k+2}m=4\\cdot2^{k}m=4n\\qquad\\forall n\\in\\mathbb N. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus **every** bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le4n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Sharpness of the constant\n\nDefine a function \\(f\\) by  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n4 &\\text{if }n=2,\\\\[2mm]\n2^{\\,v_{2}(n)+2} &\\text{if }n\\ge4\\text{ is even},\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(v_{2}(n)\\) is the exponent of \\(2\\) in the prime factorisation of \\(n\\).\n\nWe verify that (9) satisfies the bonza condition.\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\) odd.* Then \\(f(a)=1\\) and (1) holds trivially.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\) even.* Write \\(a=2^{k}m\\) with \\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\) odd.  \nFor \\(a=2\\) we have \\(f(a)=4\\); a direct check shows that \\(4\\mid b^{2}-f(b)^{4}\\) for every \\(b\\) (odd \\(b\\) give \\(b^{2}\\equiv1\\pmod4\\); even \\(b\\) give both terms divisible by \\(4\\)).  \n\nAssume now \\(a\\ge4\\); then \\(f(a)=2^{k+2}\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.1: \\(b\\) odd.*  Here \\(f(b)=1\\).  By LTE for the odd base \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\nv_{2}\\bigl(b^{a}-1\\bigr)=v_{2}(b-1)+v_{2}(b+1)+k-1\\ge3+k-1=k+2,\n\\]\n\nso \\(2^{k+2}\\mid b^{a}-1\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.2: \\(b\\) even.*  Write \\(b=2^{\\ell}r\\) with \\(\\ell\\ge1\\) and \\(r\\) odd.  \nIf \\(b=2\\) (i.e. \\(\\ell=1,r=1\\)), then \\(f(b)=4\\) and\n\n\\[\nb^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}=2^{2^{k}m}-4^{\\,2^{k+2}}\n=2^{2^{k}m}-2^{2^{k+3}}.\n\\]\n\nBoth exponents are at least \\(k+2\\); the 2\u2011adic valuation of the difference is \\(\\min(2^{k}m,2^{k+3})\\ge k+2\\), so the required divisibility holds.\n\nIf \\(b\\ge4\\) (hence \\(\\ell\\ge2\\)), then \\(f(b)=2^{\\ell+2}\\).  Compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nb^{a}&=(2^{\\ell}r)^{2^{k}m}=2^{\\ell2^{k}m}r^{2^{k}m},\\\\\nf(b)^{\\,f(a)}&=(2^{\\ell+2})^{2^{k+2}}=2^{\\ell2^{k+2}+2^{k+3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA=\\ell2^{k}m,\\qquad B=\\ell2^{k+2}+2^{k+3}=2^{k+2}(\\ell+2).\n\\]\n\nThe difference equals \\(2^{\\min(A,B)}\\times\\) (an odd integer), because the two terms have distinct 2\u2011adic exponents and the odd factor \\(r^{2^{k}m}\\) is coprime to the power of two.  Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are both multiples of \\(2^{k}\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min(A,B)\\ge2^{k}\\ge k+2.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(2^{k+2}\\mid b^{a}-f(b)^{\\,f(a)}\\).\n\nAll possibilities for \\((a,b)\\) have been covered, so the function (9) is bonza.\n\nFor a power of two \\(n=2^{k}\\) with \\(k\\ge2\\) we have \\(f(n)=2^{k+2}=4n\\); thus the ratio \\(f(n)/n\\) attains the value \\(4\\).  Consequently the constant \\(4\\) cannot be lowered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nEvery bonza function satisfies \\(f(n)\\le4n\\) for all positive integers \\(n\\), and the function defined in (9) shows that the factor \\(4\\) is best possible.  Hence the smallest real constant with the required property is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]"
